Abstract:
Modality is one of the less well-understood--and therefore undervalued--aspects of polyphony. Despite more than half a century of research into the 'modes of polyphony', many questions about their nature, evolution and meaning lack definitive answers. However, there is a very substantial body of relevant primary sources dating from the fifteenth to the eighteenth century (and even beyond), so many indeed that the question arises whether the systematic application of computational methods can shed light on some of these issues.

To set the scene, I will briefly discuss modality as detailed in Sebastian z Felsztyna's Opusculum musice (1517), followed by an overview of the main contested aspects of modality. The main part of my talk will then be about computational approaches to various kinds of relevant data, organised into several levels.

The first level deals with the sources. The Polymode database that I created to collect these now describes nearly 1000 textual and musical sources. Many of these sources contain modal cycles, sets of compositions that systematically go through all modes (whether 8, 12, or maybe another number). Data about these cycles form the second layer of my approach: the approximately 750 cycles in Polymode allow various aspects of the history of polyphonic modality to be visualised.

The third level is that of the compositions in the cycles, as well as those named in the textual sources. This is very much a work in progress, but such data will enable us to find patterns in, for example, finals, scales, and transpositions of the modes.

Scores, and by extension encodings, form the fourth level. Numerous aspects of modality can be uncovered by a systematic analysis of those compositions. So far analysis of cadences, for example, has mostly been done by hand: to do so computationally, on a large scale, would be quite helpful for our understanding.
The final level is that of musical audio. It is unrealistic to expect that enough encodings will be available for this type of research any time soon. But the quality of polyphonic audio transcription is improving fast. This raises the question of what audio analysis could contribute to our understanding of modality. This too is a work in progress, but the first results are quite encouraging.

The greatest challenge of digital musicology is, in my opinion, translating computational results to musical insights. In my closing words, I will reflect on my attempts to do so for polyphonic modality. Hopefully, something can be learnt from these that can be generalised to the application of computational methods to other open issues in musicological research.

Bio:
Frans Wiering is a (retired) Associate Professor at the Music Information Computing group of the Department of Information and Computing Sciences of Utrecht University (Netherlands). His PhD thesis ‘The Language of the Modes' (1995) investigates the richness and variety of modality in 16th- and 17th-century polyphony. His research is in computational musicology, music information retrieval and interactive technologies. Recently he published about the acceptance of new technologies in music research (‘What Do Musicologists Do All Day’, with Charles Inskip), user-centred design in corpus creation (CORSICA), and big data analysis of tonal structures in early music (CANTOSTREAM, with Mirjam Visscher).
